  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munder><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>Bromo</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>ethylpentane</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Single</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>enantiomer</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></munder><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><munderover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>reaction</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>.&#160;</mo><mi>NaOH</mi></mrow></munderover><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>ethylpentanol</mi></math></p><p>In the above reaction the chiral carbon is not involving in the reaction as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;reaction takes place via formation of transition state but not through formation of intermediate.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0003_a17f473ceaac.png\" style=\"width: 491px; height: 197px;\" /></p><p>In the above reaction, bromine attached to chiral carbon, and it is involving in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;reaction. Hence, the alcohol formed in inversion configuration.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0003_6fde1b3e4a0f.png\" style=\"width: 759px; height: 214px;\" /></p><p>The substrate is undergoing&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;reaction. It takes place via formation of carbocation. Carbocation as planar, hence, the alcohol product formed is racemic mixture as nucleophile can attack from both the planes.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0003_2dbfee3f486d.png\" style=\"width: 677px; height: 132px;\" /></p><p>The substrate is undergoing&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;reaction. It takes place via formation of carbocation. Carbocation as planar, hence, the two alcohol products are formed as nucleophile can attack from both the planes but the substrate&nbsp;already having the chiral centre, the mixture formed is diastereomeric mixture.<br />&nbsp;</p></blockquote>"
